Unix, the greatest operating system ever created, served tens of thousands of
users with a kernel that fit in under 100 kB of core. This repository aims to
bring the Seventh Edition kernel up to date with C99 to run on hardware
available nowadays, following these principles:

- No Gratuitous Changes: Leave code as it was in 1979 unless it cannot work.
- Documentation Over Implementation: Write explanations, not code.
- Small Is Beautiful: Less code means fewer bugs.


Getting Started
-------------------------

Prerequisites on Debian, Ubuntu, or WSL2:

    sudo apt install build-essential bison flex gcc-arm-none-eabi \
        binutils-arm-none-eabi gcc-arm-linux-gnueabihf qemu-user-static \
        binfmt-support qemu-system-arm

Build the system and run it under Qemu:

    make
    make qemu


References
-------------------------

- Ritchie, Dennis M., and Ken Thompson. "The UNIX time-sharing system."
  Communications of the ACM 17.7 (1974): 365-375.
- Lions, John. "A commentary on the sixth edition Unix operating system."
  Department of Computer Science, The University of New South Wales (1977).
- Bach, Maurice J. The design of the UNIX operating system. Prentice-Hall, Inc.,
  1986.
